---
  import DocLayout from '../../../components/DocLayout.astro';
  import Icon from '../../../components/Icon.astro';
  import { withBase } from '../../../lib/url';
---

<DocLayout
  title="Installation"
  description="Install Sarthi Studio on Linux, Windows, macOS, Android, and VS Code. One page per platform links to the dedicated download pages."
>
  <p>
    Install Sarthi Studio from the <a href={withBase('/download/')}>downloads section</a>.
    Every release ships a <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code>; verify the checksum
    before you install. Each platform has its own dedicated download page
    with the exact formats and installation steps.
  </p>

  <h2>Which page should I use?</h2>
  <div class="platform-grid">
    <div class="platform-row">
      <span class="platform-row-icon"><Icon name="terminal" size={18} /></span>
      <div class="platform-row-body">
        <strong>Linux</strong>
        <p>Debian/Ubuntu package, AppImage, or portable tarball.</p>
        <a href={withBase('/download/linux/')}>Linux download page →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="platform-row">
      <span class="platform-row-icon"><Icon name="monitor" size={18} /></span>
      <div class="platform-row-body">
        <strong>Windows</strong>
        <p>Per-user MSI or portable zip. WebView2 ships with Windows 10/11.</p>
        <a href={withBase('/download/windows/')}>Windows download page →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="platform-row">
      <span class="platform-row-icon"><Icon name="laptop" size={18} /></span>
      <div class="platform-row-body">
        <strong>macOS</strong>
        <p>Universal Apple Silicon + Intel build. Unsigned — Gatekeeper steps below.</p>
        <a href={withBase('/download/macos/')}>macOS download page →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="platform-row">
      <span class="platform-row-icon"><Icon name="smartphone" size={18} /></span>
      <div class="platform-row-body">
        <strong>Android</strong>
        <p>Companion client: direct router mode or Desktop Host mode.</p>
        <a href={withBase('/download/android/')}>Android download page →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="platform-row">
      <span class="platform-row-icon"><Icon name="code" size={18} /></span>
      <div class="platform-row-body">
        <strong>VS Code</strong>
        <p>The workspace inside your editor, with an embedded server.</p>
        <a href={withBase('/download/vscode/')}>VS Code download page →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Installing on several machines</h2>
  <p>
    The same release can be installed on every machine you use. Linux users
    may prefer the Debian package (menu entry plus <code>SAARTHI-STUDIO</code>
    command); AppImage and tarballs are portable and need nothing else. On
    Windows, the MSI is per-user; the portable zip needs no installer at all.
    On macOS, the universal build runs on Apple Silicon and Intel, and first
    launch needs the right-click → Open step because the build is unsigned.
  </p>

  <h2>Cross-platform workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Once installed, the workflow is the same everywhere: start the local
    service, connect a model (router or local), open a project, and run an
    agent. The UI is identical in the native window, a browser tab
    (<code>SAARTHI-STUDIO --browser</code>), and the VS Code extension.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
